  "methods": ["product rule", "factorising the derivative"],
  "statement": "Differentiate $y=\\dfrac{x^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+x^{3}}}$ with respect to $x$.",
  "solution": "Write $y=x^{2}(1+x^{3})^{-1/2}$. By the product and chain rules,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{dy}{dx}&=2x(1+x^{3})^{-1/2}+x^{2}\\left(-\\tfrac12\\right)(1+x^{3})^{-3/2}(3x^{2})\\\\\n&=2x(1+x^{3})^{-1/2}-\\tfrac{3x^{4}}{2}(1+x^{3})^{-3/2}\\\\\n&=(1+x^{3})^{-3/2}\\left[2x(1+x^{3})-\\tfrac32x^{4}\\right]\\\\\n&=(1+x^{3})^{-3/2}\\left(2x+2x^{4}-\\tfrac32x^{4}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{x(4+x^{3})}{2(1+x^{3})^{3/2}}.\n\\end{align*}",
  "solutionStatus": "full",
  "hints": [
    "Write the denominator as (1+x^3)^{-1/2}.",
    "Apply the product rule, then the chain rule on (1+x^3)^{-1/2}.",
    "Factor out the lowest power of (1+x^3)."
  ]
}
